export const WALLET_WALKTHROUGH = [
  {
    "title": "The Core Invariant: Double-Entry Bookkeeping and Solvency",
    "body": "Double-entry bookkeeping is the mathematical bedrock of all resilient financial engineering. In naive wallet designs, developers frequently update account balances with isolated mutations: decrementing Account A and incrementing Account B. This simplistic approach leaves systems catastrophically vulnerable to partial failures, race conditions, and phantom discrepancies where money is created or lost during transient errors.\n\nIn this digital wallet architecture, every single monetary movement is modeled as an immutable Transaction containing an atomic set of LedgerEntry records. Each entry specifies an account ID, an entry type (DEBIT or CREDIT), an immutable Money amount, a precise timestamp, and a human-readable memo. Under the double-entry accounting equation for liability accounts (such as customer digital wallets, which represent money owed by the platform to its users), a Credit increases an account balance, while a Debit decreases an account balance. Conversely, for asset accounts such as the platform's central bank cash settlement vault or treasury reserve, a Debit represents an increase in platform assets, while a Credit represents a cash outflow.\n\nThe inviolable invariant of the entire system is that for any transaction—whether a peer-to-peer transfer, a multi-currency FX exchange, an external deposit, or a merchant fee deduction—the sum of all debits must equal the sum of all credits within each currency denomination. This constraint is verified programmatically inside the Transaction constructor before any transaction can be committed to the LedgerRepository. Furthermore, the repository provides a global verifySystemSolvency() audit mechanism that aggregates every ledger entry across the entire history of the system, confirming that the net delta across all accounts is strictly zero. Because transactions are append-only and immutable, the current balance of any account can always be deterministically reconstructed by replaying its historical ledger entries, providing an unalterable audit trail suitable for regulatory compliance."
  },
  {
    "title": "Balance Locking and Deadlock-Free Canonical Ordering",
    "body": "Financial ledgers face relentless concurrency pressure: thousands of simultaneous transfers, top-ups, recurring subscriptions, and merchant settlements execute across shared accounts. If two concurrent threads attempt reciprocal transfers between the same pair of accounts—Thread 1 transferring from Account A to Account B while Thread 2 transfers from Account B to Account A—a naive locking implementation that locks the source account first and the destination account second will inevitably suffer from circular wait deadlocks.\n\nTo eliminate deadlocks unconditionally without relying on coarse global locks or heavy database transaction isolation levels, this architecture enforces Canonical Lock Acquisition Ordering. Every Account owns a dedicated, fair ReentrantLock. When a transfer or two-phase hold capture involves two distinct accounts, WalletService compares their unique string account IDs lexicographically:\n\n```java\nprivate void lockInOrder(Account a, Account b) {\n    int cmp = a.accountId().compareTo(b.accountId());\n    if (cmp < 0) {\n        a.lock().lock();\n        b.lock().lock();\n    } else if (cmp > 0) {\n        b.lock().lock();\n        a.lock().lock();\n    } else {\n        a.lock().lock();\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBecause all concurrent threads acquire account locks in the exact same global order regardless of transfer direction, circular wait condition (Coffman's fourth deadlock condition) is mathematically impossible. Furthermore, balances are encapsulated: mutations to availableBalance and heldBalance can only be performed when the calling thread actively holds the account's lock, verified via lock.isHeldByCurrentThread(). Readers accessing availableBalance() or totalBalance() acquire and release the lock cleanly, preventing dirty reads while allowing non-overlapping transfers between disjoint accounts to execute in complete parallel."
  },
  {
    "title": "Two-Phase Reservation: Holds, Captures, and TTL Expirations",
    "body": "Modern digital payment platforms must support pre-authorization flows common in e-commerce, ride-hailing, hotel bookings, and point-of-sale systems. In these scenarios, a customer initiates an order, and the platform must guarantee that funds remain available until the merchant fulfills the order hours or days later. If the wallet simply checked the balance at checkout without reserving it, the customer could spend those same funds elsewhere, causing the eventual capture to fail with insufficient funds.\n\nTo solve this, our wallet implements a robust Two-Phase Hold and Capture mechanism:\n\n1. Phase One (Authorization / Hold): The createHold() method locks the customer account, verifies that availableBalance >= holdAmount, and atomically decrements availableBalance while incrementing heldBalance by the exact hold amount. A PaymentHold entity is created with an ACTIVE status, a creation timestamp, and a time-to-live expiration timestamp (expiresAt). Crucially, the customer's total balance (available + held) remains unchanged, but their available balance is reduced, preventing double-spending.\n\n2. Phase Two (Settlement / Capture): When the merchant fulfills the order, captureHold() is invoked. It acquires locks on both the customer and merchant accounts using canonical ordering, confirms that the hold is in ACTIVE status and has not expired, debits the held balance on the customer account (without touching available balance), and credits the merchant's available balance. A balanced HOLD_CAPTURE transaction with corresponding LedgerEntry records is committed to the ledger, and the hold is transitioned to CAPTURED.\n\n3. Phase Two Alternate (Void / Release): If the customer cancels the order or the merchant declines the item, releaseHold() releases the hold: heldBalance is decremented and returned directly to availableBalance, marking the hold as RELEASED.\n\n4. Expiration Sweeper: Stale holds whose TTL has elapsed (expiresAt <= now) cannot remain locked indefinitely. The expireStaleHolds(Instant now) method scans active holds, atomically marks expired holds as EXPIRED, and releases held funds back into the customer's available balance."
  },
  {
    "title": "Multi-Currency Conversions and FX Clearing Pools",
    "body": "Cross-border digital commerce demands seamless multi-currency support. Users holding USD wallets frequently purchase goods priced in EUR, GBP, or INR. In a strict double-entry ledger, money in one currency cannot simply be directly credited into an account denominated in a different currency because debits and credits must balance per currency denomination.\n\nOur design solves this through Foreign Exchange (FX) Clearing Settlement Accounts. The ExchangeRateService maintains thread-safe bidirectional currency conversion rates. When Alice transfers 100.00 USD to Charlie, whose wallet is denominated in EUR with an exchange rate of 0.85 EUR per USD, the transferMultiCurrency() method coordinates a two-legged double-entry transaction:\n\n- Leg 1 (Source Currency - USD): Alice's USD wallet is debited 100.00 USD. The system's USD FX Clearing Pool account (sys-fx-settlement-usd) is credited 100.00 USD. Net USD movement across all ledger entries is strictly zero ($100 - $100 = 0).\n\n- Leg 2 (Destination Currency - EUR): The system's EUR FX Clearing Pool account (sys-fx-settlement-eur) is debited 85.00 EUR. Charlie's EUR wallet is credited 85.00 EUR. Net EUR movement across all ledger entries is strictly zero (€85 - €85 = 0).\n\nBoth currency legs are combined into a single atomic Transaction of type FX_TRANSFER containing four balanced ledger entries. This ensures complete accounting integrity: the treasury can inspect the balances of its FX clearing accounts at any time to monitor foreign exchange exposure and rebalance currency liquidity across banking partners without any unbacked currency discrepancies."
  },
  {
    "title": "Idempotency Architecture: Defending Against Duplicate Retries",
    "body": "In distributed networks, client timeouts and connection drops are inevitable. A mobile app may send a payment request, but before receiving the HTTP 200 response, cellular connectivity drops. The client or payment gateway will automatically retry the request. Without idempotency protection, network retries would trigger duplicate debits, charging the customer twice for a single order.\n\nThis system implements an enterprise Idempotent Receiver Pattern using IdempotencyRecord entries stored in the LedgerRepository. Every mutating API call requires a client-generated unique idempotencyKey (typically a UUIDv4). When a request is received:\n\n1. Payload Hash Calculation: The service constructs a canonical SHA or composite string hash of the request parameters (e.g. transfer:fromAcc:toAcc:amount:currency).\n\n2. State Check: The repository checks if the key already exists:\n   - If absent: An IdempotencyRecord is inserted atomically via putIfAbsent() in status PROCESSING. The transaction proceeds.\n   - If present with status COMPLETED: The service verifies that the incoming request payload hash matches the original request. If identical, the service immediately returns the previously completed Transaction without re-executing any ledger operations or altering balances.\n   - If present with status PROCESSING: A concurrent duplicate request is actively executing; the service throws an exception or rejects the concurrent duplicate to avoid race conditions.\n   - If present with a different payload hash: A client has erroneously reused the same idempotency key for two different transactions. The service rejects the call with an IdempotencyConflictException, preventing subtle state corruption.\n\nUpon successful transaction execution, the idempotency record is updated with status COMPLETED and the transaction ID. If an unexpected error occurs during execution, the record is marked FAILED, freeing the client to retry cleanly."
  },
  {
    "title": "Auditing, Immutability, and System Solvency Verification",
    "body": "Traditional databases often store account balances as mutable numerical columns in SQL tables, exposing them to silent data corruption, erroneous UPDATE queries without WHERE clauses, or race conditions that evade detection until quarterly audits.\n\nIn our digital wallet architecture, the ledger is modeled as an immutable, append-only journal. Accounts maintain cached availableBalance and heldBalance counters purely for fast O(1) balance lookups and low-latency pre-authorization checks. However, the authoritative source of truth is always the underlying sequence of LedgerEntry records.\n\nThe LedgerRepository provides two critical audit functions:\n1. calculateAccountNetBalance(String accountId): Reconstructs an account's true net balance by iterating over all historical ledger entries tied to that account, summing all credits and subtracting all debits. In our test suite, this computed audit balance is asserted to match the account's totalBalance() down to the exact cent.\n2. verifySystemSolvency(): Performs a global reconciliation across all transactions in the repository. It groups every ledger entry by currency and verifies that the sum of all debits equals the sum of all credits for every active currency. If a bug ever permitted an unbalanced entry into the ledger, verifySystemSolvency() would immediately flag the discrepancy. This guarantees complete financial solvency and zero unbacked money creation across the entire lifecycle of the wallet."
  },
  {
    "title": "Failure Modes, Race Conditions, and High-Throughput Scaling",
    "body": "Real-world financial systems must survive high concurrency, sudden traffic spikes, and hardware failures. This section analyzes the primary failure modes and the architectural safeguards designed into the system:\n\n1. The 'Hot Account' Problem: When thousands of customers make payments to a single merchant (e.g. during a flash sale or concert ticket release), the merchant's account lock could become a severe concurrency bottleneck. While canonical lock ordering guarantees deadlock freedom, contending threads queue on the merchant's lock. In enterprise deployments, this is solved by partitioning the merchant's ledger into multiple virtual settlement sub-accounts and aggregating them periodically via asynchronous roll-up batches.\n\n2. Negative Balances and Overdrafts: Under high concurrency, two simultaneous requests might attempt to debit an account with a $100 balance. Because each transfer acquires the account lock and verifies availableBalance >= amount under the lock before modifying state, only one request succeeds; the second request throws an IllegalStateException('Insufficient funds') and leaves the balance intact.\n\n3. Distributed Event Sourcing Migration: While this in-memory Java implementation uses ConcurrentHashMap and ReentrantLocks, the domain model is designed for seamless translation to distributed databases. The Transaction and LedgerEntry entities map directly to Kafka event streams and Postgres append-only ledger tables with optimistic concurrency control (OCC) or partitioned actor models (e.g. Akka or virtual actors in Orleans)."
  }
];

export const WALLET_CLASS_DIAGRAM = "classDiagram\n  class Currency {\n    <<enumeration>>\n    USD\n    EUR\n    GBP\n    JPY\n    INR\n  }\n  class AccountType {\n    <<enumeration>>\n    USER_WALLET\n    MERCHANT\n    TREASURY\n    FX_SETTLEMENT\n    FEE_COLLECTION\n  }\n  class EntryType {\n    <<enumeration>>\n    DEBIT\n    CREDIT\n  }\n  class TransactionType {\n    <<enumeration>>\n    DEPOSIT\n    WITHDRAWAL\n    TRANSFER\n    FX_TRANSFER\n    HOLD_CAPTURE\n    FEE\n  }\n  class HoldStatus {\n    <<enumeration>>\n    ACTIVE\n    CAPTURED\n    RELEASED\n    EXPIRED\n  }\n  class Money {\n    -long amount\n    -Currency currency\n    +of(long, Currency) Money\n    +add(Money) Money\n    +subtract(Money) Money\n    +isGreaterThan(Money) boolean\n  }\n  class Account {\n    -String accountId\n    -String ownerId\n    -Currency currency\n    -AccountType type\n    -long availableBalance\n    -long heldBalance\n    -ReentrantLock lock\n    +credit(long) void\n    +debit(long) void\n    +hold(long) void\n    +releaseHold(long) void\n    +debitHeld(long) void\n  }\n  class LedgerEntry {\n    -String entryId\n    -String transactionId\n    -String accountId\n    -EntryType type\n    -Money amount\n    -Instant timestamp\n    -String memo\n  }\n  class Transaction {\n    -String transactionId\n    -String idempotencyKey\n    -TransactionType type\n    -List~LedgerEntry~ entries\n    -Instant timestamp\n    +isBalanced() boolean\n  }\n  class PaymentHold {\n    -String holdId\n    -String accountId\n    -Money amount\n    -HoldStatus status\n    -Instant createdAt\n    -Instant expiresAt\n    +markCaptured() void\n    +markReleased() void\n    +markExpired() void\n  }\n  class ExchangeRateService {\n    -Map~String, Double~ rates\n    +setRate(Currency, Currency, double) void\n    +getRate(Currency, Currency) double\n    +convert(Money, Currency) Money\n  }\n  class LedgerRepository {\n    -Map~String, Account~ accounts\n    -Map~String, Transaction~ transactions\n    -Map~String, List~LedgerEntry~~ accountEntries\n    -Map~String, PaymentHold~ holds\n    -Map~String, IdempotencyRecord~ idempotencyRecords\n    +verifySystemSolvency() boolean\n    +calculateAccountNetBalance(String) long\n  }\n  class WalletService {\n    -LedgerRepository repo\n    -ExchangeRateService fxService\n    -Account treasuryUSD\n    -Account fxSettlementUSD\n    -Account fxSettlementEUR\n    +deposit(String, String, Money, String) Transaction\n    +withdraw(String, String, Money, String) Transaction\n    +transfer(String, String, String, Money, String) Transaction\n    +transferMultiCurrency(String, String, String, Money, String) Transaction\n    +createHold(String, String, Money, Duration, String) PaymentHold\n    +captureHold(String, String, String, String) Transaction\n    +releaseHold(String, String) void\n    +expireStaleHolds(Instant) int\n  }\n\n  Account --> Currency\n  Account --> AccountType\n  Money --> Currency\n  LedgerEntry --> EntryType\n  LedgerEntry --> Money\n  Transaction --> TransactionType\n  Transaction \"1\" *-- \"2..*\" LedgerEntry\n  PaymentHold --> HoldStatus\n  PaymentHold --> Money\n  WalletService --> LedgerRepository\n  WalletService --> ExchangeRateService";

export const WALLET_SEQUENCE_DIAGRAM = "sequenceDiagram\n  autonumber\n  actor Client as Client App\n  participant Service as WalletService\n  participant Repo as LedgerRepository\n  participant SrcAcc as Source Account\n  participant DstAcc as Dest Account\n\n  Client->>Service: transfer(idempotencyKey, srcId, dstId, 150 USD)\n  Service->>Repo: getIdempotency(idempotencyKey)\n  Note over Service, Repo: Key absent - register PROCESSING state\n  Service->>Repo: getAccount(srcId), getAccount(dstId)\n  Note over Service: Lock ordering by account ID (lexicographical)\n  Service->>SrcAcc: lock()\n  Service->>DstAcc: lock()\n  Service->>SrcAcc: debit(15000)\n  Service->>DstAcc: credit(15000)\n  Note over Service: Build double-entry balanced transaction\n  Service->>Repo: saveTransaction(tx with DEBIT src and CREDIT dst)\n  Service->>Repo: completeIdempotency(key, txId)\n  Service->>DstAcc: unlock()\n  Service->>SrcAcc: unlock()\n  Service-->>Client: Transaction (TX-TRF, Status: SUCCESS)";

