export const API_GATEWAY_WALKTHROUGH = [
  {
    "title": "Gateway Architecture: The Three-Phase Extensible Filter Pipeline",
    "body": "Modern cloud-native systems decompose applications into dozens or hundreds of autonomous microservices. Exposing internal services directly to external clients introduces immense friction: each service would have to independently re-implement TLS termination, authentication, DDoS protection, rate limiting, CORS headers, and tracing. An API Gateway serves as the single reverse-proxy entry point for all external traffic, centralizing cross-cutting infrastructure concerns.\n\nAt the heart of this API Gateway is an extensible Three-Phase Filter Pipeline modeled after enterprise gateways like Spring Cloud Gateway, Netflix Zuul 2, and Envoy:\n\n1. PRE Phase: Filters that execute before the request is routed to an upstream backend. Typical responsibilities include path resolution, protocol translation, authentication token verification, client identification, rate limit evaluation, and request payload validation.\n\n2. ROUTE Phase: Filters responsible for translating the virtual gateway path into an upstream physical destination, rewriting URLs, applying prefix stripping, dispatching HTTP calls over the network, and capturing upstream response status codes and bodies.\n\n3. POST Phase: Filters that execute after upstream responses are received (or after early termination). These filters decorate outgoing responses with telemetry headers (X-Request-Id, X-Gateway-Latency-Ms), enforce security policies (Content-Security-Policy, X-Frame-Options), compress payloads, and emit asynchronous metrics and audit logs.\n\nEach GatewayFilter declares its FilterType and an integer order(). Inside FilterPipeline, filters are grouped by phase and sorted in ascending order. This guarantees deterministic execution sequencing—for example, ensuring RouteResolutionFilter (order 10) always runs before AuthFilter (order 100), which in turn runs before RateLimitFilter (order 200)."
  },
  {
    "title": "Pre-Routing Phase and Early Request Context Short-Circuiting",
    "body": "A critical architectural capability of any production-grade gateway is Early Short-Circuit Termination. When a request fails authentication (e.g. invalid bearer token) or exceeds rate limits (HTTP 429), it must be rejected immediately at the gateway perimeter. Allowing unauthenticated or throttled requests to propagate further down the pipeline or invoke upstream microservices wastes network bandwidth, CPU cycles, and backend thread pool capacity.\n\nThe RequestContext entity coordinates this behavior across all pipeline filters:\n\n```java\npublic void terminate(int statusCode, String body) {\n    this.terminated = true;\n    this.response.setStatusCode(statusCode);\n    this.response.setBody(body);\n}\n```\n\nWhen any PRE filter calls ctx.terminate(statusCode, body):\n- The FilterPipeline immediately breaks out of the PRE phase loop.\n- The entire ROUTE phase is skipped completely—no upstream backend connection is opened or invoked.\n- The pipeline transitions directly to the POST phase.\n\nExecuting POST filters even on terminated requests is an essential design decision: clients receiving a 401 Unauthorized or 429 Too Many Requests still require standard tracing headers (X-Request-Id), security headers (X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff), and accurate rate limit reset headers (Retry-After, X-RateLimit-Reset). Furthermore, the AuditLoggingFilter must log the rejected request for security monitoring and abuse detection."
  },
  {
    "title": "Authentication, Token Verification, and Cryptographic Signatures",
    "body": "The gateway acts as the security enforcement perimeter for the internal network. Microservices positioned behind the gateway can safely assume that any incoming request has already had its identity cryptographically verified and its permissions checked against route requirements.\n\nThe TokenValidator provides dual authentication mechanisms:\n1. API Key Authentication: For machine-to-machine integrations and external developer APIs, the gateway supports pre-shared API keys supplied via the X-API-Key header. The validator maintains a concurrent registry mapping active keys to authorized subjects and assigned roles (e.g. MOBILE_CLIENT, PARTNER_INTEGRATION).\n\n2. Bearer Token Verification: For end-user sessions and mobile apps, the gateway validates structured tokens formatted as:\n   `<subject>:<comma-separated-roles>:<epochSecondExpires>:<signature>`\n   The signature is computed over the payload components using a secret signing key. When a request arrives with an Authorization: Bearer <token> header, AuthFilter extracts the raw token and verifies:\n   - Token structure and component completeness.\n   - Cryptographic signature match, ensuring the token was issued by an authorized identity provider and has not been tampered with in transit.\n   - Expiration timestamp, rejecting expired credentials with a descriptive 401 Unauthorized response.\n\nOnce verified, AuthFilter constructs an immutable AuthContext and stores it in RequestContext.attributes under the key AUTH_CONTEXT. It then performs Role-Based Access Control (RBAC): if the matched Route requires specific roles (e.g. ADMIN), the filter verifies that authContext.hasRole(requiredRole) is true. If the user lacks the role, the gateway terminates immediately with HTTP 403 Forbidden."
  },
  {
    "title": "Rate Limiting Algorithms: Token Bucket versus Sliding Window",
    "body": "Rate limiting protects backend microservices from denial-of-service attacks, cascading failures, and resource exhaustion. Different API endpoints require different throttling dynamics. This gateway implements two classic rate limiting algorithms behind a unified RateLimiter interface:\n\n1. Token Bucket Algorithm (TokenBucketRateLimiter):\n   A bucket has a fixed maximum capacity and refills continuously at a constant refillTokensPerSecond rate. When a request arrives, the algorithm calculates the tokens accumulated since the last refill based on elapsed nanoseconds:\n   `tokens = min(capacity, tokens + elapsedSeconds * refillRate)`\n   If at least one token is available, it is decremented and the request is permitted. The Token Bucket algorithm is ideal for public web APIs because it naturally accommodates bursty client behavior: a client can fire a quick burst of requests up to the bucket's capacity, after which requests are strictly smoothed to the refill rate.\n\n2. Sliding Window Algorithm (SlidingWindowRateLimiter):\n   The Sliding Window algorithm records timestamps of successful requests in a double-ended queue (ArrayDeque) per client key. When a new request arrives at time now:\n   - Timestamps older than (now - windowSizeMillis) are evicted from the head of the deque.\n   - If the remaining size is less than maxRequests, the current timestamp is appended and the request is allowed.\n   - Otherwise, the request is blocked.\n\nThe Sliding Window algorithm eliminates the edge-burst vulnerability of fixed-window counters (where twice the allowed quota can slip through at window boundary transitions), making it ideal for strict security-sensitive operations such as login attempts and payment submissions. Both implementations use ReentrantLocks per client key in a ConcurrentHashMap to guarantee thread safety without global lock contention."
  },
  {
    "title": "Reverse Proxy Routing: Longest-Prefix Matching and Path Stripping",
    "body": "A key function of an API Gateway is decoupled URI management: clients interact with clean, versioned public URLs like /api/v1/orders/123, while backend services operate on internal service routes like /orders/123 hosted across dynamic hostnames.\n\nThe RoutingTable stores configured Route definitions and implements Longest-Prefix Matching. When new routes are registered, RoutingTable sorts them in descending order of pathPrefix.length():\n- Route 1: /api/v1/users/orders -> order-service\n- Route 2: /api/v1/users -> user-service\n- Route 3: /api/v1 -> core-service\n\nWhen an incoming request for /api/v1/users/orders/42 arrives, the longest-prefix sorting guarantees that Route 1 is matched rather than the more generic Route 2 or Route 3.\n\nFurthermore, Route entities support path stripping via the stripPrefix configuration flag. When true, RoutingFilter strips the route's path prefix before dispatching to the upstream backend. For instance, a request to /api/v1/orders/items/42 routed with prefix /api/v1/orders will be forwarded to the upstream service as /items/42. Routes also support custom request header injection (addHeaders), allowing the gateway to forward environment tags, internal routing flags, or tenant identifiers to upstream handlers without exposing them to external clients."
  },
  {
    "title": "Post-Routing Transformations: Correlation Tracing and Security Headers",
    "body": "Once the upstream backend returns a response (or when a pre-filter terminates early), the request enters Phase Three: POST filters. In this phase, HeaderTransformFilter and AuditLoggingFilter execute to ensure observability, traceability, and defense-in-depth:\n\n1. Distributed Tracing Correlation IDs:\n   Every incoming request is checked for an existing X-Request-Id header. If absent, the gateway generates a fresh UUID and attaches it to both the request context and the outgoing GatewayResponse. Upstream services and client applications can log this correlation ID, enabling end-to-end distributed transaction tracing across complex microservice graphs.\n\n2. Latency Measurement:\n   RequestContext captures startTimeMs when instantiated. HeaderTransformFilter computes the total elapsed time:\n   `latencyMs = System.currentTimeMillis() - ctx.startTimeMs()`\n   and attaches it to the response header X-Gateway-Latency-Ms. This allows client monitoring teams to distinguish between network gateway overhead and upstream service latency.\n\n3. Security Headers Injection:\n   The gateway enforces baseline browser security policies by automatically injecting headers into every outgoing response:\n   - X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff (prevents MIME-type sniffing attacks)\n   - X-Frame-Options: DENY (defends against clickjacking in embedded iframes)\n\n4. Structured Audit Logging:\n   AuditLoggingFilter records an AuditRecord capturing timestamp, client IP, authenticated subject ID, HTTP method, request path, final status code, and latency. In enterprise environments, this stream feeds directly into SIEM systems (e.g. Splunk, Datadog) for anomaly detection and compliance reporting."
  },
  {
    "title": "Production Considerations: Resilience, Circuit Breaking, and Scaling",
    "body": "Deploying an API Gateway in high-throughput production environments introduces architectural challenges that span resilience, state management, and horizontal scalability:\n\n1. Circuit Breaking & Upstream Fault Isolation:\n   If an upstream microservice begins experiencing elevated error rates or high latencies, continuing to send traffic will exhaust gateway thread pools and degrade the entire platform. Production gateways incorporate Circuit Breakers (such as the Martin Fowler Circuit Breaker state machine: CLOSED, OPEN, HALF_OPEN). When failure thresholds are exceeded, the gateway fails fast, returning cached fallback responses or HTTP 503 Service Unavailable without tying up network sockets.\n\n2. Distributed State for Rate Limiting:\n   The in-memory token bucket and sliding window limiters implemented here operate per JVM instance. In a horizontally scaled cluster behind a Layer-4 load balancer (e.g. AWS NLB or Google Cloud Armor), rate limiting state must be coordinated. In enterprise deployments, this is accomplished by offloading bucket counters to Redis clusters using atomic Lua scripts or sliding log sets with TTL expiration.\n\n3. Asynchronous Non-Blocking I/O:\n   While this Java 17 standard library implementation uses thread-per-request dispatching, high-scale production gateways (e.g. Envoy, Netty-based Spring Cloud Gateway) utilize non-blocking event loops (Reactor / Epoll). Java 21 Virtual Threads (Project Loom) provide the ideal modern evolution: allowing synchronous-looking, easily readable filter code to scale to millions of concurrent connections with minimal memory footprint."
  }
];

export const API_GATEWAY_CLASS_DIAGRAM = "classDiagram\n  class HttpMethod {\n    <<enumeration>>\n    GET\n    POST\n    PUT\n    DELETE\n    PATCH\n    OPTIONS\n    HEAD\n  }\n  class FilterType {\n    <<enumeration>>\n    PRE\n    ROUTE\n    POST\n  }\n  class GatewayRequest {\n    -String path\n    -HttpMethod method\n    -Map~String, List~String~~ headers\n    -Map~String, String~ queryParams\n    -String body\n    -String clientIp\n    -Instant timestamp\n    +header(String) String\n  }\n  class GatewayResponse {\n    -int statusCode\n    -Map~String, List~String~~ headers\n    -String body\n    -Instant timestamp\n    +setHeader(String, String) void\n    +addHeader(String, String) void\n  }\n  class RequestContext {\n    -GatewayRequest request\n    -GatewayResponse response\n    -Map~String, Object~ attributes\n    -boolean terminated\n    -long startTimeMs\n    +terminate(int, String) void\n    +isTerminated() boolean\n  }\n  class GatewayFilter {\n    <<interface>>\n    +type() FilterType\n    +order() int\n    +shouldFilter(RequestContext) boolean\n    +filter(RequestContext) void\n  }\n  class FilterPipeline {\n    -List~GatewayFilter~ preFilters\n    -List~GatewayFilter~ routeFilters\n    -List~GatewayFilter~ postFilters\n    +addFilter(GatewayFilter) void\n    +execute(RequestContext) void\n  }\n  class TokenValidator {\n    -Map~String, AuthContext~ apiKeyRegistry\n    -String signingSecret\n    +validateToken(String) AuthContext\n    +generateToken(String, Set~String~, long) String\n  }\n  class RateLimiter {\n    <<interface>>\n    +tryAcquire(String, int) RateLimitResult\n  }\n  class TokenBucketRateLimiter {\n    -long capacity\n    -double refillTokensPerSecond\n    -Map~String, Bucket~ buckets\n    +tryAcquire(String, int) RateLimitResult\n  }\n  class SlidingWindowRateLimiter {\n    -long windowSizeMillis\n    -int maxRequests\n    -Map~String, Window~ windows\n    +tryAcquire(String, int) RateLimitResult\n  }\n  class Route {\n    -String id\n    -String pathPrefix\n    -String targetServiceUrl\n    -boolean stripPrefix\n    -boolean authRequired\n    -Set~String~ requiredRoles\n    -Map~String, String~ addHeaders\n  }\n  class RoutingTable {\n    -List~Route~ routes\n    +addRoute(Route) void\n    +findRoute(String) Route\n  }\n  class UpstreamBackend {\n    -Map~String, ServiceHandler~ mockServices\n    +registerService(String, ServiceHandler) void\n    +dispatch(String, String, GatewayRequest) GatewayResponse\n  }\n  class ApiGateway {\n    -RoutingTable routingTable\n    -FilterPipeline pipeline\n    -UpstreamBackend backend\n    -TokenValidator tokenValidator\n    -AuditLoggingFilter auditLogger\n    +handle(GatewayRequest) GatewayResponse\n  }\n\n  GatewayFilter <|.. RouteResolutionFilter\n  GatewayFilter <|.. AuthFilter\n  GatewayFilter <|.. RateLimitFilter\n  GatewayFilter <|.. RoutingFilter\n  GatewayFilter <|.. HeaderTransformFilter\n  GatewayFilter <|.. AuditLoggingFilter\n  RateLimiter <|.. TokenBucketRateLimiter\n  RateLimiter <|.. SlidingWindowRateLimiter\n  FilterPipeline \"1\" *-- \"*\" GatewayFilter\n  ApiGateway --> FilterPipeline\n  ApiGateway --> RoutingTable\n  ApiGateway --> UpstreamBackend\n  ApiGateway --> TokenValidator";

export const API_GATEWAY_SEQUENCE_DIAGRAM = "sequenceDiagram\n  autonumber\n  actor Client as External Client\n  participant GW as ApiGateway\n  participant Pipe as FilterPipeline\n  participant Auth as AuthFilter\n  participant Limiter as RateLimitFilter\n  participant RouteF as RoutingFilter\n  participant Backend as UpstreamBackend\n  participant PostF as HeaderTransformFilter\n\n  Client->>GW: handle(GatewayRequest)\n  GW->>Pipe: execute(RequestContext)\n  Pipe->>Auth: filter(ctx) [PRE, order=100]\n  Note over Auth: Verify Bearer token signature & roles\n  Auth-->>Pipe: Context enriched with AuthContext\n  Pipe->>Limiter: filter(ctx) [PRE, order=200]\n  Note over Limiter: TokenBucket.tryAcquire(clientId)\n  Limiter-->>Pipe: Rate check passed (Remaining: 4)\n  Pipe->>RouteF: filter(ctx) [ROUTE, order=500]\n  RouteF->>Backend: dispatch(targetService, strippedPath)\n  Backend-->>RouteF: GatewayResponse(200 OK, JSON)\n  RouteF-->>Pipe: Response set in Context\n  Pipe->>PostF: filter(ctx) [POST, order=900]\n  Note over PostF: Inject X-Request-Id, Latency & Security Headers\n  PostF-->>Pipe: Post-processing complete\n  Pipe-->>GW: Execution finished\n  GW-->>Client: GatewayResponse(200 OK with Headers)";

