      "body": "Once the upstream backend returns a response (or when a pre-filter terminates early), the request enters Phase Three: POST filters. In this phase, HeaderTransformFilter and AuditLoggingFilter execute to ensure observability, traceability, and defense-in-depth:\n\n1. Distributed Tracing Correlation IDs:\n   Every incoming request is checked for an existing X-Request-Id header. If absent, the gateway generates a fresh UUID and attaches it to both the request context and the outgoing GatewayResponse. Upstream services and client applications can log this correlation ID, enabling end-to-end distributed transaction tracing across complex microservice graphs.\n\n2. Latency Measurement:\n   RequestContext captures startTimeMs when instantiated. HeaderTransformFilter computes the total elapsed time:\n   `latencyMs = System.currentTimeMillis() - ctx.startTimeMs()`\n   and attaches it to the response header X-Gateway-Latency-Ms. This allows client monitoring teams to distinguish between network gateway overhead and upstream service latency.\n\n3. Security Headers Injection:\n   The gateway enforces baseline browser security policies by automatically injecting headers into every outgoing response:\n   - X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff (prevents MIME-type sniffing attacks)\n   - X-Frame-Options: DENY (defends against clickjacking in embedded iframes)\n\n4. Structured Audit Logging:\n   AuditLoggingFilter records an AuditRecord capturing timestamp, client IP, authenticated subject ID, HTTP method, request path, final status code, and latency. In enterprise environments, this stream feeds directly into SIEM systems (e.g. Splunk, Datadog) for anomaly detection and compliance reporting."
